---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContactBand from '../../components/layout/ContactBand.astro';
import FixedExperimentalRecreation from '../../components/compound-growth/FixedExperimentalRecreation.astro';
import ResearchEvidenceInteractions from '../../components/compound-growth/ResearchEvidenceInteractions.astro';
import MonthlyCompoundGrowthExplorer from '../../components/compound-growth/AnnualCompoundGrowthExplorer.astro';
import '../../styles/compound-growth.css';
import '../../styles/compound-growth-page.css';
---

<BaseLayout title="Compound Growth & Retirement Investing" description="Can identical financial projections produce different judgments depending on how they are shown?" canonicalPath="/work/compound-growth">
<article class="cg-case-study" data-compound-growth-page>
<div class="cg-suite" data-compound-growth-suite>
<header class="cg-page-hero">
 <p class="cg-page-hero__credential">MSc · Marketing Research &amp; Behavioural Science · University of Ottawa</p>
 <h1>Compound Growth &amp; Retirement Investing</h1>
 <p class="cg-page-hero__lede">Across five randomized experiments, I tested whether identical retirement-growth projections produce different judgments when shown as a numerical table or a line graph.</p>
 <dl class="cg-page-meta" aria-label="Research program at a glance"><div><dt>Program</dt><dd>5 randomized experiments</dd></div><div><dt>Population</dt><dd>Young adults who had not begun investing for retirement</dd></div></dl>
 <div class="cg-page-hero__grid"><div class="cg-research-question"><p class="cg-page-label">Research question</p><h2><span class="rq-line rq-line-1">Can the same retirement projection produce different</span> <span class="rq-line rq-line-2">judgments when shown as a table or line graph?</span></h2><p class="cg-main-finding"><strong>Main finding:</strong> Line graphs made compound-growth acceleration more apparent than tables.</p></div></div><div class="cg-hero-pathway" role="group" aria-labelledby="hero-pathway-title"><p class="cg-page-label" id="hero-pathway-title">Statistical pathway</p><div class="cg-pathway" role="group" aria-label="Experiment 4 statistical pathway: randomized line-graph presentation followed by measured perceived compound-growth acceleration, growth confidence, retirement-goal achievability, and planned contributions."><div class="cg-pathway__step cg-pathway__step--randomized"><span class="cg-pathway__index">01</span><span class="cg-pathway__kind">Randomized</span><strong>Line graph presentation</strong></div><span class="cg-pathway__connector" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 24" focusable="false"><path d="M2 12H36"></path><path d="M30 6L37 12L30 18"></path></svg></span><div class="cg-pathway__step"><span class="cg-pathway__index">02</span><span class="cg-pathway__kind">Measured</span><strong>Greater perceived compound-growth acceleration</strong></div><span class="cg-pathway__connector" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 24" focusable="false"><path d="M2 12H36"></path><path d="M30 6L37 12L30 18"></path></svg></span><div class="cg-pathway__step"><span class="cg-pathway__index">03</span><span class="cg-pathway__kind">Measured</span><strong>Stronger confidence in investment growth</strong></div><span class="cg-pathway__connector" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 24" focusable="false"><path d="M2 12H36"></path><path d="M30 6L37 12L30 18"></path></svg></span><div class="cg-pathway__step"><span class="cg-pathway__index">04</span><span class="cg-pathway__kind">Measured</span><strong>Retirement feels more achievable</strong></div><span class="cg-pathway__connector" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 44 24" focusable="false"><path d="M2 12H36"></path><path d="M30 6L37 12L30 18"></path></svg></span><div class="cg-pathway__step"><span class="cg-pathway__index">05</span><span class="cg-pathway__kind">Measured</span><strong>Higher planned<br>contributions</strong></div></div></div>
</header>
<FixedExperimentalRecreation />
<section class="cg-narrative cg-narrative--representation" aria-labelledby="representation-title"><div class="cg-representation-head"><div class="cg-narrative__heading"><p class="cg-kicker">Representation</p><h2 id="representation-title">Why a table and graph may feel different</h2></div><p class="cg-representation-note">The research question was not whether graphs are universally “better.” It was whether representation changes what people perceive in the same financial projection.</p></div><div class="cg-narrative-grid"><div><h3>Table</h3><p>To notice acceleration, the viewer has to compare values across different intervals and recognize that the gains are getting larger.</p></div><div><h3>Line graph</h3><p>The same values form a curve that gets steeper over time, making the accelerating pattern more visible.</p></div></div></section>
<ResearchEvidenceInteractions />
<MonthlyCompoundGrowthExplorer />
<section class="cg-narrative" aria-labelledby="limitations-title"><div class="cg-narrative__heading"><p class="cg-kicker">Limitations</p><h2 id="limitations-title">What the research does not show</h2></div><div class="cg-limitations"><p><strong>Real-world investing behaviour</strong> — The studies measured hypothetical monthly contribution selections, not real deposits.</p><p><strong>Population generalizability</strong> — Participants were young adults who had not begun investing for retirement.</p><p><strong>Causal ordering</strong> — The intermediate variables were measured rather than independently manipulated, so the statistical models do not prove that each step caused the next.</p><p><strong>Universal display superiority</strong> — Experiment 5 left the exact visual mechanism partly unresolved, and tables can still be better for precise value lookup.</p></div></section>
</div>
</article>
<ContactBand />
</BaseLayout>

